## Migration: EventProcessor to .NET 8

### Problems with the original code on .NET 8

| API | Issue |
|-----|-------|
| `Thread.Abort()` | Throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` (SYSLIB0006) |
| `Thread.Interrupt()` | Obsolete (SYSLIB0046 in .NET 9), unreliable for waking `WaitOne` |
| `ManualResetEvent.WaitOne()` | Not cancellation-aware — can't be woken cooperatively |
| Race condition | Multiple `Enqueue` calls between `WaitOne`/`Reset` lose signals; only one item dequeued per wake |

### Patterns identified

- **Cancellable work loop** → `CancellationToken` checked in loop
- **Blocking call interruption** → `WaitHandle.WaitAny` with `token.WaitHandle`

---

### Migrated code

```csharp
public class EventProcessor : IDisposable
{
    private readonly CancellationTokenSource _cts = new();
    private readonly ManualResetEventSlim _newEvent = new(false);
    private readonly Queue<string> _queue = new();
    private readonly object _lock = new();
    private Thread? _thread;

    public void Start()
    {
        _thread = new Thread(RunLoop);
        _thread.Start();
    }

    private void RunLoop()
    {
        var token = _cts.Token;
        while (!token.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            // Block until signaled OR cancelled
            WaitHandle.WaitAny(new[] { _newEvent.WaitHandle, token.WaitHandle });

            if (token.IsCancellationRequested)
                break;

            // Drain all queued items (fixes original race condition)
            while (true)
            {
                string? item;
                lock (_lock)
                {
                    if (_queue.Count == 0)
                        break;
                    item = _queue.Dequeue();
                }
                Handle(item);
            }

            _newEvent.Reset();
        }
    }

    public void Enqueue(string item)
    {
        lock (_lock) { _queue.Enqueue(item); }
        _newEvent.Set();
    }

    public void Stop()
    {
        _cts.Cancel();               // wakes WaitAny via token.WaitHandle
        _thread?.Join(5000);         // wait for graceful exit
        // No fallback — cooperative cancellation means the loop exits
    }

    public void Dispose()
    {
        Stop();
        _cts.Dispose();
        _newEvent.Dispose();
    }

    private void Handle(string item) { /* your logic */ }
}
```

---

### Key pitfalls to watch for

| Pitfall | Mitigation |
|---------|-----------|
| `Thread.Abort` has no replacement — you can't force-kill a thread | The loop **must** check the token; if `Handle()` is long-running, pass the token into it too |
| `ManualResetEvent.WaitOne()` ignores cancellation | Use `WaitHandle.WaitAny` with `token.WaitHandle` as a second handle |
| Original code had a lost-signal race (multiple enqueues, one dequeue) | Drain the queue in a loop after each wake |
| `CancellationTokenSource` is `IDisposable` | Implement `IDisposable` on the class and dispose it |
| `ManualResetEventSlim` is preferred over `ManualResetEvent` | Lighter weight; use `.WaitHandle` only for the `WaitAny` call |

### Even simpler alternative (if you can go fully modern)

Replace the entire class with `System.Threading.Channels`:

```csharp
public class EventProcessor : IDisposable
{
    private readonly Channel<string> _channel = Channel.CreateUnbounded<string>();
    private readonly CancellationTokenSource _cts = new();
    private Task? _worker;

    public void Start() => _worker = Task.Run(RunLoopAsync);

    private async Task RunLoopAsync()
    {
        await foreach (var item in _channel.Reader.ReadAllAsync(_cts.Token))
        {
            Handle(item);
        }
    }

    public void Enqueue(string item) => _channel.Writer.TryWrite(item);

    public async Task StopAsync()
    {
        _channel.Writer.Complete();
        if (_worker != null)
            await _worker.WaitAsync(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5));
    }

    public void Dispose() => _cts.Dispose();
    private void Handle(string item) { /* your logic */ }
}
```

The `Channel<T>` version eliminates manual locking, wait handles, and thread management entirely — it's the idiomatic .NET 8 approach for producer/consumer queues.
